[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the reported failure. End-to-end reachability from the “near” floor to the “far end” is working normally. Topology-wide checks show pc0 (20.1.1.10) and pc12 (120.1.1.10) can ping each other bidirectionally with 0% loss and ~1 ms RTT, which validates both forward and return paths. On the OSPF/EIGRP boundary, r1 correctly resolves traffic to 120.1.1.10 via the OSPF area 3 neighbor r11 (ip route get 120.1.1.10 → next hop 111.1.1.2 on r1), and r12 has 120.0.0.0/8 directly attached for pc12. The OSPF domain across r1–r3–r4–r6 and the EIGRP island (r11–r12–r13) are stable with single, consistent next-hops; no mutual recursion or oscillating routes are present in the RIB/FIB snapshots I collected.

Specifically, there are no OSPF area range/summarization statements configured on any ABRs that could “summarize an area into itself,” and the virtual links (r4↔r8) are correctly defined by router-IDs, not creating self-referential summaries. r11 redistributes connected/EIGRP into OSPF area 3 as designed, and no redistribution back into EIGRP is configured on r12/r13, avoiding feedback loops.

Root cause: Not a network fault; the network is currently healthy and forwarding normally. The earlier observation of “packets going in circles” is not substantiated by present routing state or data-plane tests.

Corrective fix: None required. If an intermittent issue was observed earlier, capture timestamps and traceroutes during the event; otherwise operations can proceed as normal. === END ===